course: dictionary
lesson: 1186
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can tell which meaning is meant by reading the words around it, which is the context. «Goats are curious browsers that nibble a bit of everything» is about animals, so browsers here means animals that eat leaves and shoots.
explain: «When Ruby types a web address, the browser asks that server for the page.» Ruby is typing on a computer, so a browser must be a computer program. Ask yourself: which meaning fits the words around it? Then try that meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: «A cookie is a small file a website saves in your browser to remember things like your login or basket.» Words like website, file and login are the clues that tell you a browser is the program you use to look at websites.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A browser is a computer program used to look at websites on the internet, such as Chrome, Safari or Firefox.
fact: A browser can also be a person who looks around a shop, or at things slowly, without planning to buy.
fact: In nature, a browser is an animal that eats leaves and shoots from trees and bushes, like a giraffe or a deer.
fact: A URL is a web address, and DNS is the internet's phone book, which turns names into IP address numbers.
exercise: «Goats are curious browsers that nibble a bit of everything.» What does browsers mean here? | Paper labels | People who look around shops | Computer programs | *Animals that nibble leaves and plants
exercise: «When Ruby types a web address, the browser shows the page.» What is the browser in this sentence? | A giraffe in a field | *A computer program for looking at websites | A tin can | A person browsing in a shop
exercise: Mum says, «I was only browsing in the shop, I'm not buying anything.» What does browsing mean? | Typing a web address | Eating leaves from a tree | *Looking around slowly without planning to buy | Paying at the till
test: «A cookie is a small file a website saves in your browser to remember your login.» What is a browser in this sentence? | A shop assistant | A password | An animal that eats leaves | *A program used to look at websites
test: «The browser asks that server for the page.» What does the word page suggest here? | A sheet of paper in a book | An animal that browses | *Something shown on a website | A shop window
exam: «The deer spent the morning browsing on the young leaves at the edge of the wood.» Which meaning of browsing fits best? | Saving a file on a computer | *Eating leaves and shoots from bushes | Looking around a shop | Typing into a search box
